WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.990
Hi, this is Presh Talwalkar. In this video
we're going to talk about extending the

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:08.240
factorial function beyond the whole
numbers and described the gamma function.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:13.760
For a brief refresher, the factorial
function is a way to count the number of

00:00:13.769 --> 00:00:16.199
arrangement of objects in a line.

00:00:16.199 --> 00:00:19.320
It's often written with an exclamation
point (!).

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:25.529
So 1 factorial is 1; 2 factorial is 2
times 1, which is 2; 3 factorial is 3...

00:00:25.529 --> 00:00:31.460
times 2 times 1, which is 6; 4 factorial
is 4 times 3 times 2 times 1,

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:36.719
which is equal to 24 and in general
n factorial is equal to the product of all the whole

00:00:36.719 --> 00:00:44.010
numbers up to n. So let's play around
with the calculator and verify some of

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:51.420
these calculations. We'll start out by
trying one factorial; we'll press one and

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:59.219
then the factorial to get an answer 1.
We'll then do 2 factorial; 2 factorial,

00:00:59.219 --> 00:01:06.270
that's equal to 2. Three factorial is equal
to 6, and 4 factorial is equal to 24.

00:01:06.869 --> 00:01:12.180
So everything is as we expected but
let's say we put in a fraction like .5.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.299
We actually get a result!

00:01:15.299 --> 00:01:19.560
This isn't an error: you actually get a
number, and that's pretty interesting,

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:26.540
and let's try and figure out what this
number is. Let's say we square the number,

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:34.960
and just because I know what the value
is what i'm going to multiply it by 4,

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:41.740
and we end up with 3.14159! This is
something we recognize; this is equal to pi!

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:47.360
So, quite remarkably we found out that
one-half factorial is equal to the

00:01:47.369 --> 00:01:52.740
square root of pi divided by two! So where does 
this come from because it has nothing to do

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:56.960
with the definition that we have just
explained which we were all taught in school.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:03.270
So to explain how one half factorial is
equal to that value will start out by

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:04.950
graphing the factorial function.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:13.410
So 1 factorial is 1, 2 factorial is 2, 3
factorial is 6, and 4 factorial is 24.

00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:19.530
So these are the integer whole values of
the factorial function and if we want to

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:23.400
extend it beyond the whole numbers we
can imagine connecting the dots.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:28.380
So one way we could extend the factorial
function we just draw a line between any

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:33.400
two dots, and that could define values for
the factorial function for the decimals.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:37.640
We could try at smoothing out the curve;
there actually infinitely many ways we

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:43.100
can smooth out the curve. So we want to
figure out a way that we can smooth out

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:48.180
the curve which has desirable properties.
So we want to come up with the factorial

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.450
function and we want the factorial of 1 --

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:56.460
we want this to be equal to 1, so it
matches with the fact that one factorial is 1.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:03:03.540
We also want f(x + 1) equal x*f(x).
This allows us to say the

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:08.940
factorial thing that each new number is
the product of the ones before it.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:14.220
The final properties we want to graph to be
smooth and one way we can do that as we

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:17.609
could say that the log of f(x)
needs to be convex.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:22.660
So that's a function - I won't get into
the details, but the reason we have this

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:27.880
is if you have these conditions there's actually
a unique solution there's one function:

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.080
the gamma function, that satisfies these
three conditions.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:37.140
So that's how we can extend the factorial
function and it's a complicated function.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:44.020
The gamma of x is equal to the integral from 0
to infinity of t to the power of x - 1

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:47.280
times e to the power of -t, dt.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:52.580
So this will converge for some values of x;

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:55.540
it won't converge for other values;

00:03:55.540 --> 00:04:02.800
but what we will do then, is we'll define
that x factorial is equal to gamma of x + 1.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.900
So this gamma function satisfies
all three of the properties to the left,

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:13.140
so since x factorial is defined this way
we can then evaluate the gamma function

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:18.400
to find out that one-half factorial is
equal to square root of pi over 2.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:23.760
So why do we care about the gamma function?
Well, this type of integral actually comes up

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.040
in many applications like quantum
physics, fluid dynamics, statistics,

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:29.440
and even number theory.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:34.340
So there's a really important reason that
we want to extend the factorial function,

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:37.180
and our calculators can
actually evaluate it.

